{#
  THE REPORT. A web page that happens to be printed.

  Reached at /actions/leadgenerator/report/view?entry=<id>, which is also how Playwright
  reaches it before calling page.pdf(). So what you see in a browser at A4 width is what a
  prospect gets, and designing it is a refresh rather than a re-render.

  IT READS AND NEVER CALCULATES. Every number here came out of report.json, which came out
  of lib/surface.mjs over a partition asserted to tile the page exactly. Adding anything up
  in this file would be a second implementation of a number somebody may argue with.

  THE ANNOTATED PAGE IS ONE IMAGE SHOWN THROUGH SEVERAL WINDOWS. CSS cannot break an image
  across printed pages — it overflows and is cut, or shrinks to nothing — and a homepage
  screenshot is thousands of pixels tall (jersey.com is 16,368). So each sheet holds a box
  with the image as its background, offset by one sheet's worth, which is the only way the
  block labels stay readable.

  WHAT MUST SURVIVE ANY REDESIGN: the caveats. A number this tool could not measure is
  reported as unmeasured, and a report that quietly drops that is not this tool's report.
#}
{% set pct = { } %}
{#
  A percentage, or an em dash where there is nothing to say.

  `<0.1%` AND NOT `0.0%` for anything present but smaller than the rounding can show.
  kohde.agency paints rgb(220,220,226) across 288 pixels — about seventeen square — which
  is 0.0033% of its page and rounded to a flat nought. A colour in a list of colours on the
  page cannot be none of the page, and a reader asked why it was there. Rounding a real
  measurement down to zero is the same fault as reporting an unmeasured region as empty.
#}
{% macro pc(n) %}
    {%- if n is null -%}—
    {%- elseif n > 0 and n * 100 < 0.05 -%}&lt;0.1%
    {%- else -%}{{ (n * 100)|number_format(1) }}%
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{# MB TO ONE PLACE, KB BELOW A MEGABYTE.

   BINARY, AND IN STEP WITH tools/audit/lib/bytes.mjs:136, which divides by 1048576 and
   calls the result MB. Decimal would be the more defensible unit and would match Chrome's
   network panel, but the same bytes are printed by the capture CLI through that function:
   two formatters means the PDF saying 4.5 MB while the console says 4.3 MB for one page.
   If this moves, that moves with it. #}
{% macro bytes(n) %}
    {%- if n is null -%}—
    {%- elseif n >= 1048576 -%}{{ (n / 1048576)|number_format(1) }}mb
    {%- else -%}{{ (n / 1024)|number_format(0) }}kb
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{# ONE BAR, TWO SCORES, and they are not the same kind of number: Speed is PageSpeed
   divided by ten, Technical is this tool's own bands. Drawn identically because a reader
   compares them anyway, so the difference is stated in the labels rather than hidden in
   the styling. #}
{% macro scorebar(n) %}
    <div class="r-score">
        {# ALWAYS A DECIMAL. A score of 8 and a score of 8.4 should not print in different
           shapes: `8` reads as a rounder, cheaper number than `8.0`, and beside `6.7`
           on the same cover it looks like a different kind of measurement. #}
        <div class="r-score-n">{{ n|number_format(1) }}<span>/10</span></div>
        <div class="r-score-bar" style="--r-fill: {{ n * 10 }}%"></div>
    </div>
{% endmacro %}

{# THE SAME BAR, WITH A NAME OVER IT. The cover stacks three of these and the number alone
   cannot say which is which, but a label BESIDE the bar pushed it 141px right and 141px
   short of the one the sections print — measurably not the same object. Above it, the row
   underneath is scorebar() itself, so the two cannot drift. #}
{% macro scoreline(label, n) %}
    <div class="r-scoreline">
        <p class="r-scoreline__label">{{ label }}</p>
        {{ _self.scorebar(n) }}
    </div>
{% endmacro %}
{% import _self as r %}

{# A4 at 96dpi less the margin — the box an image has to fit in. Kept in step with
   PAGE in tools/audit/lib/pdf.mjs; if one moves the other has to. #}
{% set sheet = { width: 704, height: 1033 } %}
{% set shotWidth = report.width ?? 1440 %}
{% set shotHeight = report.height ?? 0 %}
{% set scaled = shotHeight * (sheet.width / shotWidth) %}
{% set sheets = min(12, max(1, (scaled / sheet.height)|round(0, 'ceil'))) %}

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>{{ report.url }} homepage audit</title>
    <style>
        /* RELATIVE, LIKE THE SCREENSHOT, AND FOR THE SAME REASON. craft.vite.asset returns an
           absolute URL at the site's hostname, and the browser printing this runs inside the
           container where that name does not resolve: every face failed ERR_FAILED and the
           PDF printed in a system fallback that looks near enough to pass. Stripping the
           origin leaves the path, which resolves against whichever host loaded the page.
           An earlier `ignoreHTTPSErrors` fixed a certificate error and was reported as
           fixing this; it did not, and nothing said so.
        
           THE SITE'S OWN TYPE, resolved through craft.vite.asset so the content hash in the
           built filename is never written down here — a rebuild changes it, and a
           hardcoded path would fail by silently falling back to a system font, which on a
           PDF nobody would notice until a prospect had one.

           Declared here rather than by pulling in the whole site stylesheet: this page has
           its own layout and does not want the site's, and a print document that inherits
           a nav's rules is a document fighting its own CSS. */
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonMono';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-leicht.woff2')|replace({'/^https?:\\/\\/[^\\/]+/': ''}) }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 500;
            font-style: normal;
            /* `block`, not `swap`: this is printed, not read while it loads, and a swap
               means Chromium can print the fallback if the font lands a moment late. */
            font-display: block;
        }
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonMono';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-halbfett.woff2')|replace({'/^https?:\\/\\/[^\\/]+/': ''}) }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 600;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: block;
        }

        /* The site's sans, for the cover alone. Declared here for the same reason the mono
           is: this sheet does not load the site's stylesheet, so the faces it uses have to be
           named in it. Matches the JonSonSans block in build/scss/theme/_typography.scss. */
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonSans';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/Baikal-ExpandedBold.woff2')|replace({'/^https?:\\/\\/[^\\/]+/': ''}) }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 600;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: block;
        }
        @font-face {
            font-family: 'JonSonSans';
            src: url('{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/Baikal-ExpandedLight.woff2')|replace({'/^https?:\\/\\/[^\\/]+/': ''}) }}') format('woff2');
            font-weight: 500;
            font-style: normal;
            font-display: block;
        }

        /* Matches $g-font-secondary in build/scss/theme/_setup.scss. */
        :root { --r-mono: 'JonSonMono', ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
            --r-sans: 'JonSonSans', system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }

        @page { size: A4; margin: 45px; }
        * { box-sizing: border-box; }
        body { margin: 0; font: 500 11px/1.55 var(--r-mono); color: #111; }

        /* On screen, stand the sheets on a grey field so the printed page is visible while
           it is being designed. Neither rule survives into the PDF. */
        @media screen {
            body { background: #e9e9ec; padding: 24px; }
            /* WHERE THE PAPER ACTUALLY ENDS. 794x1123 is A4 at 96dpi, but it is a MIN-height:
               a sheet holding more than a page grows, silently, and the preview stops being
               the thing it is previewing. kohde.agency's first sheet is 1775px — 652px past
               the paper — and nothing on screen said so.

               So the boundary is drawn every 1123px rather than the content being clipped
               to it. Clipping would hide the overflow, which is the one thing worth seeing.
               Screen only: this rule does not exist in the PDF. */
            .r-sheet { width: 794px; min-height: 1123px; padding: 45px; margin: 0 auto 24px;
                background: #fff repeating-linear-gradient(to bottom,
                    transparent 0 1121px, rgba(224,46,26,.5) 1121px 1123px);
                box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.2); }
        }
        /* NOTHING IS CUT IN HALF EXCEPT THE SCREENSHOT, which is already sliced to the page
           before it gets here. The first sheet holds more than one page of content — kohde
           runs to 1,775px against A4's 1,123 — so it flows, and where it flows is what these
           rules decide.

           `tr` AND NOT `table`: the Segments table is taller than a page on its own, so
           avoiding a break inside the whole table would push it to a fresh page and overflow
           anyway. Keeping each ROW whole is the strongest rule that can always be honoured.

           A row whose Note is a twelve-colour palette can still be taller than a page. Then
           the browser breaks it regardless, which is the right failure: a rule that cannot
           be met is not enforced, it is ignored. */
        @media print {
            /* FULL BLEED, AND ONLY ON THE COVER. With no page margin the navy reaches all
               four edges; the sheet takes the 45px back as padding so the type sits where it
               always did. `:first` keeps every other page on its 45px margin, which is what
               the running head draws in and what PAGE.margin in lib/pdf.mjs slices the
               screenshot against — neither has to move. The head is clipped on page one for
               want of a band, which on a cover is the right answer anyway. */
            @page :first { margin: 0; }

            .r-sheet { break-after: page; padding: 0; }
            /* THE COVER NEEDS A HEIGHT TO CENTRE AGAINST. `min-height: 1123px` is in the
               screen block only, so in print a sheet is as tall as its content — and a flex
               column with no free space has nothing for `margin: auto` to eat, which put the
               whole cover back at the top of the page while the preview looked right.
               `100vh` in paged media is the page box, and the @page margins take their own
               space out of it. */
            /* AFTER `.r-sheet`, and that is not a tidiness point. The cover carries both
               classes and the two rules have the same specificity, so whichever is written
               last wins: with this above, `.r-sheet { padding: 0 }` won and the cover printed
               flush to all four edges with the type touching the paper. */
            .r-cover { min-height: 100vh; padding: 45px; }
            .r-sheet:last-child { break-after: auto; }
            tr, li, .r-score, table { break-inside: avoid; }
            /* `table` as well as `tr`, because a table split between its second and third
               row reads as two tables. A table taller than a page cannot honour this and the
               browser breaks it anyway, which is the right failure: a rule that cannot be met
               is ignored rather than enforced badly. */
            /* A heading stranded at the foot of a page is worse than a short page. */
            h2, h3 { break-after: avoid; }
            .r-score, table { break-before: avoid; }
            /* Carried on to the next page, a table still says what its columns are. */
            thead { display: table-header-group; }
        }

        /* The grey the h3s carry. Set on the element rather than on the cover, so both
           addresses in the document — the cover's and the content sheet's — read the
           same way without either one being special-cased. */
        h1 { font-size: 25px; font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 2px; letter-spacing: -0.01em;
            color: #5e5e5e; }
        /* Sections stand apart: a heading landing hard against the table above it reads
           as a row of that table rather than the start of a new one. */
        h2 { font-size: 19px; font-weight: 600; margin: 22px 0 8px; }
        h3 { font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600; margin: 24px 0 9px; color: #5e5e5e; }
        /* Margins collapse, so the space under an h2 that is followed by an h3 is the
           h3's 24px and not the h2's. A subheading belongs to the heading above it. */
        h2 + h3 { margin-top: 10px; }
        /* Only 500 and 600 are loaded, and <b> asks for 700. Left alone the browser
           picks the nearest, which is right here but is a decision nobody made —
           and on a print document a synthesised bold is visible. */
        b { font-weight: 600; }
        .r-meta { color: #333; margin-bottom: 22px; }
        table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
        th { text-align: left; font-weight: 600; border-bottom: 1px solid #111; padding: 5px 0; }
        /* Mono is already tabular, but saying so stops a fallback ruining the column. */
        /* TOP, not the middle. A row whose Note is a twelve-item palette floats its
           label and its number halfway down the cell, so the three stop reading as
           one row. */
        td { padding: 5px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid #eee; vertical-align: top; }
        tfoot td { border-top: 1px solid #111; border-bottom: none; padding-top: 7px; }
        .r-n { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-facts { margin: 20px 0; color: #333; }
        /* A facts line belonging to a heading sits under it, not 20px away from it. */
        h2 + .r-facts { margin-top: 0; }

        /* THE SCORE BAR. The fill is a pseudo-element and the value arrives as a custom
           property, so there is no empty element in the markup whose only job is to be a
           colour. `printBackground` is on in lib/pdf.mjs, which is what makes it survive
           the print — without it the bar would be an empty grey box in the PDF and
           nowhere else. */
        .r-score { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; margin: 0 0 14px; }
        /* A FLOOR SO EVERY BAR STARTS ON THE SAME LINE. `8` is a glyph narrower than
           `9.5`, and the number shrinks to fit, so the Consistency bar began 33px left
           of the others. 90px is the widest this column can be, now that every score carries
           a decimal: `10.0` is the longest form it gets. */
        .r-score-n { min-width: 90px; font-size: 28px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1;
            letter-spacing: -0.01em; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-score-n span { font-size: 13px; color: #666; }
        /* The site's own two colours, by value rather than by class: this sheet cannot
           load build/scss without inheriting the rules it exists to avoid. Track is
           `primary 430` (.b-footer__whisper), fill is `primary 700` (.b-slab--p700).
           If the palette moves in theme/_setup.scss, it has to be moved here too. */
        .r-score-bar { position: relative; flex: 1; height: 12px; border-radius: 999px;
            background: #b6ddff; }
        .r-score-bar::before { content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0 auto 0 0;
            width: var(--r-fill); border-radius: 999px; background: #0c243c; }
        .r-speed td:nth-child(1), .r-speed th:nth-child(1) { width: 32%; padding-right: 16px; }
        .r-speed td:nth-child(2), .r-speed th:nth-child(2) { width: 20%; padding-right: 16px; }
        .r-speed { margin-bottom: 26px; }
        /* A swatch has to carry its own outline: a near-white chip on a white sheet is
           otherwise invisible, which is exactly the case this section is about. */
        .r-swatches, .r-drift { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 0; }
        td .r-swatches, td .r-drift { margin: 0; }
        /* The palette as a run of swatches rather than a stack of rows. The chip is a
           pseudo-element and the colour arrives as a custom property, so the markup is
           a list of colours and not a list of colours and boxes. Each one is nowrap so
           `rgb(255, 255, 255)` never breaks across two lines mid-value. A near-white
           chip on a white sheet carries its own outline. */
        .r-swatch-inline { display: inline-block; white-space: nowrap; margin: 0 10px 3px 0;
            font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-swatch-inline::before { content: ''; display: inline-block; width: 11px; height: 11px;
            margin-right: 4px; vertical-align: -1px; background: var(--c);
            border: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.25); }
        /* The sections behind a segment's percentage, under its name. The index is the
           one lib/debug.mjs draws on the screenshot, so it is set apart rather than
           reading as part of the description. */
        .r-parts { list-style: none; margin: 4px 0 2px; padding: 0; color: #5e5e5e; font-weight: 500; }
        .r-parts li { padding: 1px 0 1px 10px; text-indent: -10px; }
        .r-parts li::before { content: '•'; margin-right: 6px; }
        .r-parts span { color: #999; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-parts em { font-style: normal; color: #b62515; }
        .r-swatches li, .r-drift li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 6px; padding: 3px 0; }
        .r-chip { display: inline-block; width: 13px; height: 13px; flex: none;
            border: 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.25); }
        .r-chip-label { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-chip-share { margin-left: auto; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
        .r-swatch--head { border-bottom: 1px solid #111; padding-bottom: 5px; font-weight: 600; }
        .r-chip--blank { border-color: transparent; }
        .r-swatch--rest .r-chip-label { color: #666; }
        .r-drift b { font-weight: 600; opacity: .5; }
        .r-none { margin: 0 0 18px; color: #333; }
        .r-caveats { margin-top: 22px; padding: 12px; background: #f6f6f6; }
        .r-caveats li { margin-bottom: 6px; }

        /* One image, several windows onto it. `background-position` walks down it a sheet
           at a time; the box is a fixed height so a short last slice does not stretch. */
        /* THE COVER. A column: label and address at the top, their first screen below it,
           the date pushed to the foot. The capture is 1440x900 and is drawn at the sheet's
           own width, so it keeps its proportions whatever the paper is. */
        /* Bottom-aligned: the stack sits on the foot of the sheet and the air goes above
           it, so a short findings list and a long one both end on the same line. */
        /* `margin: auto 0` on the head is what does it: in a flex column an auto margin eats
           the free space, so the address centres in whatever is left above the foot while the
           foot stays on the bottom edge. A short findings list and a long one then end on the
           same line, and the difference is absorbed above where nobody looks. */
        /* The head centres in the space above the foot, and is then nudged down. Centring on
           the SHEET instead was tried and overshot: it put the title on the page's midpoint,
           a long way below where it had been. This is the original position plus 60px. */
        /* THE DARK SHEET. primary 700 and primary 430 from build/scss/theme/_setup.scss —
           the same two the score bars are drawn in, swapped round: on navy the navy fill
           would vanish, so the pale blue does the filling and the track becomes a wash of
           white. Everything else on this sheet inverts with it. */
        .r-cover { display: flex; flex-direction: column;
            background: #0c243c; color: #fff; }
        .r-cover h1,
        .r-cover .r-scoreline__label,
        .r-cover .r-cover__date { color: rgba(255,255,255,.66); }
        .r-cover .r-cover__label { color: #fff; }
        .r-cover .r-score-n { color: #fff; }
        .r-cover .r-score-n span { color: rgba(255,255,255,.55); }
        .r-cover .r-score-bar { background: rgba(255,255,255,.2); }
        .r-cover .r-score-bar::before { background: #b6ddff; }
        .r-cover__head { margin: auto 0; position: relative; top: 60px; }
        .r-cover__band { flex: none; margin: -45px -45px 0; padding: 22px 45px;
            background: #fff; }
        .r-cover__logo { display: block; width: 186px; height: auto; }
        /* 55px. The one thing on the sheet set in the site's sans: it
           is the title of the document, so it is the only line that does not have to look
           like a measurement.

           `balance` ON THE WRAP, because at this size "Homepage Audit" is most of the
           column: it fits on one line here, and a longer title would otherwise break with
           one word stranded on the second. */
        .r-cover__label { margin: 0 0 16px; color: #111; font-family: var(--r-sans);
            font-size: 55px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1; letter-spacing: -0.02em;
            text-wrap: balance; }
        .r-cover__url { margin: 0 0 32px; word-break: break-all; }
        .r-cover__scores { margin: 0; }
        /* Under the address, not at the foot of the sheet: it is part of what this report
           is OF — this page, on this day — rather than a note about the document. */
        .r-cover__date { margin: 10px 0 0; color: rgba(255,255,255,.66); }

        .r-summary__head { margin-top: 0; }
        /* The label sits over the row, and the row is scorebar() untouched, so the cover's
           three scores and the one above each table are the same object to the pixel. */
        .r-scoreline { margin: 0 0 20px; }
        /* 15px and #5e5e5e: the h3 treatment, because that is what it is — the name of
           the score under it, doing the same job `Speed` does above the Speed table. */
        .r-scoreline__label { margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 15px; font-weight: 600;
            color: #5e5e5e; }
        .r-scoreline .r-score { margin: 0; }
        .r-scoreline .r-score-bar { flex: 1; }
        /* The same label the scores carry, so the cover has one voice for its headings. */
        .r-findings__head { margin-top: 26px; }
        .r-findings { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
        .r-findings li { padding: 4px 0 4px 14px; text-indent: -14px; }
        .r-findings li::before { content: '•'; margin-right: 8px; }

        .r-shot { width: {{ sheet.width }}px; height: {{ sheet.height }}px;
            {# `raw`, AND IT HAS TO BE. Twig escapes for HTML by default, so the `&` between
               this URL's parameters arrives in the stylesheet as `&amp;` — CSS does not
               decode entities, so the browser asks for `...&amp;entry=` and Craft answers
               400. The PDF then carries blank pages where the screenshot should be and
               nothing in the pipeline fails. The value is built in ReportController from an
               integer and a fixed filename; there is nothing here to inject. #}
            background-image: url('{{ annotated|raw }}');
            background-repeat: no-repeat;
            background-size: {{ sheet.width }}px auto; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

{# THE COVER. Their own first screen, because a prospect opening this should see their page
   before they see a number: it says the report was made for them rather than generated at
   them. `viewport.png` is phase 1's capture of the first 1440x900 and is the only image in
   the audit that is their page rather than our marks on top of it.

   THE SCORES ARE HERE AS WELL AS ON THEIR OWN SECTIONS, deliberately repeated. Speed is
   PageSpeed divided by ten and the other two are this tool's own bands, which is a
   difference a cover cannot carry — so each one is printed again beside the table that
   earns it, where the rows show the working.

   STATED AND NOT EXPLAINED. "52.1% of the page is routing" is a measurement; "which is why
   nobody converts" is an argument this tool cannot support, and a reader who catches one
   overreach stops believing the tables as well. #}
{% if firstScreen is defined and firstScreen %}
    <section class="r-sheet r-cover">
        {# Two groups, and the space between them is the design: the address sits in the
           middle of the sheet, the summary and the date on its foot. #}
        {# A WHITE BAND, EDGE TO EDGE. The cover pads itself by 45px so the type sits in from
           the paper; the band has to undo that on three sides to reach the edges, which is
           what the negative margins are for. `flex: none` keeps it out of the centring —
           the address still centres in what is left below it.

           THE LOCKUP IS INLINE, not an <img>. Three separate things on this sheet have
           already failed silently by asking for a file at an absolute URL the printer could
           not reach — the report page, the screenshot and the fonts. Inlined, there is
           nothing to fetch and nothing to fail. #}
        <div class="r-cover__band">
            {% include '_views/report/_lockup.svg' %}
        </div>

        <div class="r-cover__head">
            <p class="r-cover__label">Homepage Audit</p>
            <h1 class="r-cover__url">{{ report.url }}</h1>
            <p class="r-cover__date">{{ report.capturedAt|date('jS F Y') }}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="r-cover__foot">
        {# TEN POINTS EACH, THIRTY IN ALL. The overall is the three added up and put back on
           the same scale, which is the version a reader can check in their head: "23 out of
           a possible 30". It is arithmetically the mean, and the cost of that is worth
           knowing — a perfect Consistency buys back a poor Technical, so a page shipping
           4.3mb with nothing deferred still comes out around 7.

           ONLY WHEN ALL THREE ARE THERE. Averaging two and calling it a total would score a
           page out of 20 and print it as though it were out of 30.

           THE THREE THEMSELVES ARE NOT ON THE COVER. Each one is printed above the table
           that earns it — Speed over the PageSpeed metrics, Technical over the weight rows,
           Consistency over the duplicate colours — and a cover that lists them as well makes
           the reader check the same four numbers twice. #}
        {% set marks = [
            report.speed ? report.speed.score / 10 : null,
            report.technical ? report.technical.score : null,
            report.brand ? report.brand.score : null
        ]|filter(v => v is not null) %}
        <div class="r-cover__scores">
            {% if marks|length == 3 %}
                {{ r.scoreline('Overall', (marks|reduce((sum, v) => sum + v, 0) / 3)|round(1)) }}
            {% endif %}
        </div>

        {% set biggest = report.categories|first %}
        {% set heaviest = report.weight ? report.weight.byType|first : null %}
        <p class="r-scoreline__label r-findings__head">Summary</p>
        <ul class="r-findings">
            {% if biggest and biggest.share > 0 %}
                <li>{{ r.pc(biggest.share) }} of the page is <b>{{ biggest.category }}</b>,
                    across {{ biggest.blocks|length }}
                    section{{ biggest.blocks|length == 1 ? '' : 's' }}.</li>
            {% endif %}
            {% if heaviest %}
                <li>{{ r.bytes(heaviest.bytes) }} of it is <b>{{ heaviest.type }}</b>,
                    {{ r.pc(heaviest.bytes / report.weight.afterScroll.bytes) }} of the weight
                    across {{ heaviest.requests }} of {{ report.weight.afterScroll.requests }} requests.</li>
            {% endif %}
            {% if report.technical %}
                <li>
                    {%- if report.technical.shortPage -%}
                        The page is barely taller than a screen, so there is little below the fold to defer.
                    {%- elseif report.technical.deferScore >= 4 -%}
                        {{ r.pc(report.technical.deferred) }} of the weight waits until the visitor scrolls.
                    {%- else -%}
                        <b>Nothing is deferred</b>: the whole page loads before the visitor scrolls.
                    {%- endif -%}
                </li>
            {% endif %}
            {% if report.brand %}
                <li>
                    {%- if report.brand.groups -%}
                        {{ report.brand.colours }} colours on the page are
                        <b>{{ report.brand.groups == 1 ? 'a pair' : report.brand.groups ~ ' sets' }}</b>
                        a person cannot tell apart.
                    {%- else -%}
                        No two colours on the page are ones a person could confuse.
                    {%- endif -%}
                </li>
            {% endif %}
        </ul>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endif %}

<section class="r-sheet">
    <h1>{{ report.url }}</h1>
    <p class="r-meta">
        <b>URL measured on:</b> {{ report.capturedAt|date('jS F Y') }}
        &bull; <b>Page dimensions:</b> {{ report.width }}px wide by {{ report.height|number_format }}px tall
    </p>

    {% if report.speed or report.weight %}
        <h2>Performance</h2>
        {% if report.speed %}
        {# THE THRESHOLDS ARE GOOGLE'S PUBLISHED ONES, so a reader who disagrees with a
           verdict is arguing with Google rather than with this report — and the numbers
           stay checkable, because anyone can run PageSpeed themselves in a minute.

           The score inverts: for PageSpeed higher is better, for the other three lower is.
           Written out per row rather than generalised, because four rows of plain
           conditions are easier to check against Google's table than one clever rule.

           No Twig comments inside the hash below — one in an array literal 500s the page. #}
        {% set psi = report.speed.score %}
        {% set lcp = report.speed.lab.lcpMs %}
        {% set tbt = report.speed.lab.tbtMs %}
        {% set cls = report.speed.lab.cls|round(3) %}
        {% set speedRows = [
            {
                metric: 'Google PageSpeed',
                score: psi ~ '/100 Desktop',
                note: psi >= 90 ? 'Good (90 or above)' : (psi >= 50 ? 'Needs work (under 90)' : 'Poor (under 50)')
            },
            {
                metric: 'Largest Contentful Paint',
                score: lcp ~ 'ms',
                note: lcp <= 2500 ? 'Good (under 2.5s)' : (lcp <= 4000 ? 'Needs work (over 2.5s)' : 'Poor (over 4s)')
            },
            {
                metric: 'Total Blocking Time',
                score: tbt ~ 'ms',
                note: tbt <= 200 ? 'Good (under 200ms)' : (tbt <= 600 ? 'Needs work (over 200ms)' : 'Poor (over 600ms)')
            },
            {
                metric: 'Cumulative Layout Shift',
                score: cls,
                note: cls <= 0.1 ? 'Good (under 0.1)' : (cls <= 0.25 ? 'Needs work (over 0.1)' : 'Poor (over 0.25)')
            }
        ] %}
        <h3>Speed</h3>
        {{ r.scorebar((report.speed.score / 10)|round(1)) }}
        <table class="r-speed">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Metric</th>
                    <th>Score</th>
                    <th>Note</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for row in speedRows %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>{{ row.metric }}</td>
                        <td>{{ row.score }}</td>
                        <td>{{ row.note }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table>
    {% endif %}
        {% if report.weight %}
            {# THESE BANDS ARE OURS, AND SPEED'S ARE GOOGLE'S. Worth knowing the difference:
               a reader who disputes "Good (under 2.5s)" is arguing with Google, and one who
               disputes "Needs work" here is arguing with us. So the comparison is carried in
               the same breath as the verdict — at or under the median is Good, up to twice
               it Needs work, past twice it Poor — and the median is named every time, so
               the reader can see the line being drawn rather than take the word for it. #}
            <h3>Technical</h3>
            {% if report.technical %}{{ r.scorebar(report.technical.score) }}{% endif %}
            {% set total = report.weight.afterScroll.bytes %}
            {# THE ONE NUMBER IN THIS REPORT THAT IS NOT MEASURED. Everything else came off
               the capture; this is the median desktop page from HTTP Archive, and it is a
               moving target — it has risen year on year. It is stated in MB and multiplied
               to bytes so it reads the same way r.bytes() prints, and it lives here alone
               so correcting it is one edit rather than a hunt through prose. #}
            {% set medianMb = report.technical ? report.technical.medianMb : 2.3 %}
            {% set medianBytes = medianMb * 1048576 %}
            {% set vsMedian = (total - medianBytes) / medianBytes %}
            {% set weightVerdict = vsMedian <= 0.05 ? 'Good' : (vsMedian <= 1 ? 'Needs work' : 'Poor') %}
            {% set heaviest = report.weight.byType|first %}
            <table class="r-speed">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Metric</th>
                        <th>Score</th>
                        <th>Note</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>Total page weight</td>
                        <td>{{ r.bytes(total) }}</td>
                        <td>
                            {{- weightVerdict }} (
                            {%- if vsMedian|abs < 0.05 -%}
                                about the median page
                            {%- else -%}
                                {{- (vsMedian|abs * 100)|number_format(0) }}%
                                {{ vsMedian > 0 ? 'above' : 'below' }} the median page
                            {%- endif -%})
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    {# WHAT THIS ROW IS ABOUT IS LAZY LOADING, and its absence. atLoad is
                       marked before the scroll pass and afterScroll once the page has been
                       walked to the bottom, so the gap between them is what the site chose
                       to defer. kohde.agency defers more than half its weight; a page that
                       defers none makes a visitor who reads the first screen and leaves pay
                       for every image below it.

                       THE SHORT-PAGE GUARD IS NOT OPTIONAL. A page barely taller than the
                       viewport has nothing below the fold to defer, and 100% before
                       scrolling is then the correct answer rather than a fault. 900 is the
                       capture viewport in lib/capture.mjs; if that moves, this moves. #}
                    {% set deferred = 1 - (report.weight.atLoad.bytes / total) %}
                    {% set nothingBelow = report.height < 900 * 2 %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>Weight before scrolling</td>
                        <td>{{ r.bytes(report.weight.atLoad.bytes) }}</td>
                        <td>
                            {%- if nothingBelow -%}
                                Good (little below the fold to defer)
                            {%- elseif deferred >= 0.4 -%}
                                Good ({{ r.pc(deferred) }} deferred until scroll)
                            {%- elseif deferred < 0.02 -%}
                                Needs work (nothing deferred until scroll)
                            {%- else -%}
                                Needs work (only {{ r.pc(deferred) }} deferred until scroll)
                            {%- endif -%}
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    {% if heaviest %}
                        <tr>
                            <td>Heaviest type</td>
                            <td>{{ r.bytes(heaviest.bytes) }} ({{ heaviest.type }})</td>
                            {# "18 requests" ON ITS OWN MEANS NOTHING, and the row that
                               carried the total was dropped. The comparison is what the
                               number is for, so it travels with it rather than depending
                               on another row being there. #}
                            {# THE TWO PERCENTAGES ARE NOT THE SAME PERCENTAGE. 39.1% is this
                               type's share of the BYTES; its share of the requests is 18 of
                               78, which is 23%. Naming what each number counts is the only
                               thing stopping a reader doing the arithmetic and finding it
                               does not work. #}
                            <td>{{ r.pc(heaviest.bytes / total) }} of the weight,
                                {{ heaviest.requests }} of
                                {{ report.weight.afterScroll.requests }} requests</td>
                        </tr>
                    {% endif %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    {% if report.styles or report.weight %}
        <h2>Brand</h2>
        {# ONE TABLE UNDER ONE HEADING. Only the duplicates row feeds the score: a palette of
           eleven is not worse than one of six, and nothing here marks a page for the size of
           its palette or the number of its typefaces. Those are choices. #}
        <h3>Consistency</h3>
        {% if report.brand %}{{ r.scorebar(report.brand.score) }}{% endif %}
        <table class="r-speed">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Metric</th>
                    <th>Score</th>
                    <th>Note</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                    {# FONTS AS ONE ROW, not a section of their own. What survived scrutiny is
               the weight and the number of typefaces actually drawn with; "faces
               declared" counted @font-face blocks, which nobody can check against a
               network tab and which double-counts a stylesheet that declares the same
               face twice.

               NO FILE COUNT, because the census counts RESPONSES. kohde returns 13
               font responses for 8 distinct files: reCAPTCHA's Roboto arrives six
               times, five of them from cache at nought bytes. Printing "13 files"
               would be wrong, and deduplicating by URL is a change to lib/bytes.mjs
               rather than something to fudge here. The bytes are right either way. #}
            {% set fontBytes = report.weight.byType|filter(t => t.type == 'font')|first %}
            {% if fontBytes %}
                <tr>
                    <td>Fonts</td>
                    <td>{{ r.bytes(fontBytes.bytes) }}</td>
                    <td>
                {%- if report.styles and report.styles.fonts.rendered|length -%}
                    {{ report.styles.fonts.rendered|length }}
                    {{- report.styles.fonts.rendered|length == 1 ? ' family' : ' families' }}
                    on the page ({{ report.styles.fonts.rendered|map(f => f.family)|join(', ') }})
                {%- else -%}
                    No typeface was measured on the page
                {%- endif -%}
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endif %}
                {% if report.styles and report.styles.colours.palette|length %}
                    {# THE COUNT IS ONE CELL, THE PALETTE IS THE NEXT. A number on its own
                       cannot tell a palette from an accretion: eleven colours where three
                       cover most of the page is a palette with accents, eleven spread evenly
                       is a page nobody decided. The shares are what settle it, so they sit
                       in the same row as the count rather than in a section below it.

                       OF THE PAGE, not of the summed colour areas, which was the first
                       version and was a percentage of a number nobody can picture: the
                       colour areas add up to 1.02x the page on kohde.agency and 0.78x on
                       whitepaper.co.uk, so one figure meant something different on each.
                       These do not total 100%, and both directions are honest. Short of it
                       is page with no colour of its own, a transparent element over whatever
                       is behind it. Over it is elements nesting, where a panel and the box
                       inside it both count their own area.

                       A CEILING THAT DECLARES ITSELF. This listed eight and said eleven
                       beside it, and a reader counted. Whatever is cut has to be named and
                       has to carry its share, or the list disagrees with the count. #}
                    <tr>
                        <td>Primary colours</td>
                        <td>{{ report.styles.colours.total }}</td>
                        <td>
                            {%- for c in report.styles.colours.palette -%}
                                <span class="r-swatch-inline" style="--c: {{ c.colour }}">{{ c.colour }}</span>
                            {%- endfor -%}
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                {% endif %}
                {% if report.styles %}
                    {# CHECKED AND FOUND NONE IS STILL AN ANSWER. An absent row is
                       indistinguishable from a check that never ran, and on this tool that
                       distinction is the whole argument. whitepaper.co.uk is the case: its
                       closest two colours are dE 6.58 apart, so nothing groups, which is a
                       finding about the page rather than a gap in the report.

                       WHAT COUNTS AS THE SAME COLOUR is within dE 2.3 in CIELAB once alpha
                       is collapsed, the point a person stops telling two apart.
                       visionarygrid.studio declares its brand yellow twice, as
                       rgb(255,211,0) and rgb(255,210,2); jersey.com paints three near-blacks
                       a point apart. Differences nobody chose and nobody can see, which is a
                       design token entered twice. Stated and not judged: a page may layer
                       near-whites deliberately. #}
                    <tr>
                        <td>Duplicate colours</td>
                        <td>{{ report.styles.colours.sameColour|length }}</td>
                        <td>
                            {%- if report.styles.colours.sameColour|length -%}
                                <ul class="r-drift">
                                    {% for group in report.styles.colours.sameColour %}
                                        <li>
                                            {% for colour in group %}
                                                <span class="r-chip" style="background: {{ colour }}"></span>
                                                <span class="r-chip-label">{{ colour }}</span>{% if not loop.last %} <b>~</b> {% endif %}
                                            {% endfor %}
                                        </li>
                                    {% endfor %}
                                </ul>
                            {%- else -%}
                                None (no two colours within dE {{ report.styles.colours.deltaE }} of each other)
                            {%- endif -%}
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                {% endif %}
            </tbody>
        </table>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Segments</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Segment</th>
                <th class="r-n">Share of Page</th>
                <th class="r-n">Ink to Segment</th>
                <th class="r-n">First Viewport</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {% for c in report.categories %}
                <tr>
                    {# THE SECTIONS BEHIND THE PERCENTAGE. "Explainer 57.8%" is not something a
                       reader can act on; seven named sections is. The number is the one drawn
                       on the annotated screenshot, so a row in this table can be found on the
                       image. #}
                    <td><b>{{ c.category|title }}</b>
                        {%- if c.blocks|length %}
                            <ul class="r-parts">
                                {% for b in c.blocks %}
                                    {# A BLOCK THE MODEL DOUBTED SAYS SO. kohde.agency's case
                                       study card was labelled wrong at 0.6 confidence, the
                                       lowest on that page, and nothing in the report let a
                                       reader see it. Marked rather than hidden or dropped:
                                       the label is still our best answer, it is just the one
                                       to check against the picture. #}
                                    <li{% if b.unsure %} class="r-part--unsure"{% endif %}>
                                        {{- b.what ?: 'unnamed' }} <span>(Slice {{ b.n }})</span>
                                        {%- if b.unsure %} <em>unsure</em>{% endif -%}
                                    </li>
                                {% endfor %}
                            </ul>
                        {%- endif %}
                    </td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.share) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.coverage) }}</td>
                    <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(c.firstViewport) }}</td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
        {# COMPUTED, NOT PRINTED AS "100%". Both columns should come to exactly that, so a
           row that adds them up is a check on the arithmetic rather than a decoration: if
           the shares ever stop totalling, this is where it shows. It is how `explainer`
           going missing would have been caught — atkinsonsca.co.uk reported 42% of its own
           homepage and nothing said the other 58% had gone.

           The ink column is the page's own coverage and NOT the sum of the column above it,
           which would add percentages of different areas together and mean nothing. #}
        <tfoot>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Total</b></td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(report.categories|reduce((sum, c) => sum + c.share, 0)) }}</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(report.coverage) }}</td>
                <td class="r-n">{{ r.pc(report.categories|reduce((sum, c) => sum + (c.firstViewport ?? 0), 0)) }}</td>
            </tr>
        </tfoot>
    </table>





    {# THE CAVEATS ARE NOT PRINTED HERE, and this comment is the record of that decision.
       They still reach the template in report.caveats and still live in report.json; what
       was cut is showing them to a prospect. kohde.agency's only one names SwiftShader and
       ANGLE, which reads as a fault in this tool rather than a finding about their site.

       THE ONE TO WATCH IS `blankRegion` / `contentNotPainted`. Those say a region of the
       page was never measured — visionarygrid.studio has 3,600px of case study the capture
       photographed as empty yellow — and with nothing printed, its percentages silently
       exclude it and nothing on the sheet says so. If that ever turns up on a real lead,
       it belongs back on the page in a sentence a client can read. #}
</section>

{% for i in 0..(sheets - 1) %}
    <section class="r-sheet">
        <div class="r-shot" style="background-position: 0 -{{ i * sheet.height }}px;"></div>
    </section>
{% endfor %}

</body>
</html>
